From dc02f53340f28537dfb7264cd559df8cb21d4bf6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 17:22:22 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 12/23] BananaDS perf: the 2D job ring without the 3D-line and
 display arrays between the jobs (render.2d_job_stride)

A BananaRenderer::Job held its 4 KB copy of the 3D line's four quarters and its 512-byte display line inline, so a
job was ~4.9 KB and the ring of 512 jobs 2.5 MB: every job the 2D thread took was on a page of its own, its ~250
hot bytes on cache lines no stream prefetcher could have brought in, and with render.3d_share (the default at 2x)
the 4 KB copy is never written at all. rgds-sim on the head (dsstress L5, device mode): the thread's loop
(SetThreaded2D's lambda) 22.5k instructions but 0.147 ms/frame, 3.8% of the 2D thread, with 0.9k dTLB walks a frame.

Now Line3D and Display are pointers; MakeJobRing lays the jobs out with a byte stride (JobAt) and points them at
their arrays: render.2d_job_stride=compact (the default) keeps the arrays in a block of their own, so the ring the
2D thread reads is 512 x ~256 B, consecutive; =inline puts each job's arrays right behind it, the layout before.
Read once, when the ring is made (before SetThreaded2D in emu.cpp).

Exact by construction: a layout, the same bytes in the same fields. rgds-sim (dev mode, inline -> compact): the
thread's loop 0.176 -> 0.112 ms/frame on L5, 0.172 -> 0.139 on L10; the 2D thread's dTLB walks 0.9k -> 0.3k/frame;
the thread as a whole within dev mode's noise. TSan (x86, 3 runs): no data race.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
